## Eisel-Lemire decimal-to-binary64 conversion (Lemire, "Number Parsing at a
## Gigabyte per Second", 2021; the form of Go's strconv): a u64 decimal
## mantissa times a 128-bit power of ten, rounded once. It declines (none) the
## rare inputs it cannot decide, subnormals, overflow and exponents beyond the
## table, which the caller then hands to strings.parse_f64.
import memory

## Decimal exponents the table covers; OBJ coordinates sit well inside.
let smallest_exponent: i64 = -64
let largest_exponent: i64 = 64

## 10^q for q in smallest_exponent..=largest_exponent as a 128-bit mantissa,
## rounded down and normalized so its top bit is set: high word, low word.
## Each is floor(5^q * 2^s) for q >= 0 and floor(2^s / 5^-q) for q < 0, with
## s chosen for exactly 128 bits (10^q and 5^q differ only in the exponent).
